the best way to learn a language it  turns out is not to sit in a class and  recite the different verbs and nouns  a lot of research and a lot of anecdotal  evidence tells us that the best way to  learn a language  is through immersion it's to live in  another country speak and hear  only that language and let the amazing  plasticity of our brain  do its thing training is no different  we are adaptoids our bodies are designed  to adapt to our surroundings  and this is the key to unlocking perfect  performance this might sound like a  pleasant platitude  but in fact i believe it's also  supported by ever increasing evidence  let's see a few of the ways in which  lifestyle trumps training  firstly no stimulus will ever be as  profound as the environment  best case scenario you might work out  for an hour or two a day  this is nothing compared to the amount  of time many of us spend sitting in our  seats and typing  or lying on the safe for watching tv  that's the main stimulus  it should come as no surprise then that  we experience tight hip flexors  rounded shoulders and beer bellies as  i've often explained  this is not maladaptive this is  functional for the lifestyles that we  lead  this also goes some way to explaining  the seemingly inexplicable farmer  strength  they don't train to failure but they  train all day  training with huge volume will always  generate huge results  so long as the recovery is equal to the  energy expended and overload is cautious  and progressive  i think high rep calisthenics are the  perfect example of this  recently i've been championing the use  of high rep calisthenics with a  continuous time under tension  this type of training has the ability to  flood the working muscles of blood  reducing venous return as well as  maximally fatiguing all the muscle  fibers  it can permanently increase blood supply  to those muscles by capillarization  which additionally increases my nuclei  for permanently improved hypertrophy  it builds strength endurance the most  useful type of strength that allows us  to exert power for long durations  without fatiguing and failing  this is also highly protective against  injury the problem  huge repetitions of moderately  challenging movements take time  and don't fit neatly into a given  training program they work best when  spread throughout the day  like so many accounts of prisoners and  legendary old-time strongmen  the same goes for like cardio spread  throughout the day this goes a long way  to explaining the seemingly superhuman  feats of indigenous peoples  such as the tarahumara known for running  marathon-like distances  every single day but it's not just the  body that benefits from this kind of  total immersion  the brain also helps refine and improve  movement through continuous  distributed repetition this is greasing  the groove  as described by pavel tatsulin this  means repeating a movement  intermittently throughout the day  so as to reinforce neural pathways  for example you might perform a pull-up  every time you pass through a doorway  or you might practice a turkish get-up  every morning and night  strength is a skill says pavel instead  of focusing on muscle damage and  metabolic stress  instead focus on repetition so that you  develop the most flawless technique  that way you recruit only the necessary  muscle fibers with no wasted energy  you become stronger without the fatigue  training in this way has other benefits  too  this allows us to tap into something  called space repetition or distributed  practice  research shows us that when you spread  any form of learning throughout the day  you actually see more rapid learning and  better retention  this makes sense as each new session  requires you to perform the movement  from cold as it were coincidentally this  is also how you would use the movement  in real life  we don't get a nice little rehearsal  period first  not only that but each learning session  is followed by a cooldown period  during which you will experience post  activation potentiation in the relevant  neural pathways  as well as the short term plasticity we  discussed recently  in other words your brain doesn't just  reset more separate cooldowns  means more total activation  each new learning session will spike  chemicals like brain-derived  neurotrophic factor  and in the case of resistance training  it might also spike protein synthesis  growth hormone testosterone and more  you'll raise your metabolism repeatedly  too  then there's the simple fact that any  block of training will likely lead to  some fatigue  and loss of focus  strategically breaking your training up  means introducing more rest time  allowing you to start each new block  fresh and with mental clarity  what do you do during these rests though  you continue to learn in other ways  you continue to try other types of  movement you intersperse your training  with other activities  this introduces the interference effect  and research shows that when you mix up  drills rather than performing blocks of  the same type of training  you actually see a drop in performance  during those training sessions  but greater performance and retention  host training  in real life we're rarely required to  repeat a single movement over and over  without varying so why would we stand in  the corner of our gym  curling on the spot  likewise we should avoid repeating  movements exactly all together  to learn the perfect deadlift you must  practice the perfect deadlift over and  over  you must ensure that the movement is as  identical as possible  each and every time this is useful for  competition  but it's not that useful in real life or  in sports  there are no straight bars in real life  there are few flat surfaces  rarely do we have the luxury of perfect  form if you're wrestling climbing or  moving furniture  you'll need to lift from awkward angles  with varied momentum  it is by introducing variety and  confounding variables into training that  we therefore become more  ready for anything this way we develop  what nikolai bernstein refers to as  more robust neural maps movement  patterns emerge from constraints  organism environment and goal this is  dynamical systems theory  as applied to motor learning  imbalances and overuse injuries are  rarely the result of using muscles too  often  rather they are the result of using  particular movement patterns at the  exclusion  of all others tennis elbow doesn't occur  because those movements are harmful  it occurs very often because the muscles  of the forearm aren't being equally  trained  gyphosis occurs because the front is  stronger and tighter than the back and  it band pain often afflicts runners  because they rarely move in the frontal  plane and wear thick supporting shoes  movement is medicine but only if it's  sufficiently varied and balanced  just like nutrition continuous movement  can even help to encourage recovery  if you stop moving a joint to protect it  you give the body no incentive  to heal the affected area you develop  awkward compensatory movement patterns  that worsen the problem and you actively  prevent blood flow  growth hormone and protein synthesis in  the affected area  this can even go so fast to accelerate  aging a single setback often coalesces  into a multitude of problems  that become too entangled to unravel  did you know that one of the best ways  to encourage rapid recovery and growth  post training is to use light jogging  or even a very light recovery set for  the muscles you trained in the previous  workout  the same thing applies to injuries on a  grander scale  and on a similar note blood flow  restriction training can also help to  accelerate recovery  too many functional trainers will take a  reductionist approach to solving this  problem  they will write training programs that  they hope will offer perfect balance  make sure you're training external  rotation for those rotator cuffs  make sure you're 